{"reference":"1 Samuel 18","book":"1 Samuel","chapter":18,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Saul’s harmful spirit","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"In 1 Samuel 18:10-12, the book attributes the harmful spirit affecting Saul to the LORD and also says that the LORD had departed from Saul. The language should not be reduced to a single medical, psychological, spiritual, or theological diagnosis; the passage itself presents the experience in its own terms.","verse_refs":["1 Samuel 18:10-12"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_samuel_018_6a09d8ead31b47d5551cfb4a"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"A textual question about the preceding account","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The David and Goliath account exists in a substantially shorter ancient Greek form and a longer Masoretic form. How those forms are related remains disputed, so reconstructions of the literary relationship should be held cautiously.","verse_refs":["1 Samuel 17"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_samuel_018_be42d2c2abd0d7e07ecfb59c"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}